{
  "name": "Schrödinger soliton collisions",
  "description": "Saturable nonlinear Schrödinger equation — light in a self-focusing, saturable medium (photorefractive crystals, atomic vapours). The field ψ = p + iq disperses, and focuses itself where it is bright; the two balance in 2D solitons ('light bullets'), and saturation stops them collapsing. It's computed by exact split steps (FFT for the dispersion, a local phase rotation for the nonlinearity), so it's reversible and conserves total power to float precision. Brightness is the intensity n = |ψ|², tint shows the real part p (so the phase). Top: two solitons in phase collide head-on — they attract and fuse. Bottom: the same pair a half-cycle out of phase repel and bounce apart. The relative phase is invisible in the intensity but decides the outcome: this is the information solitons carry. A sponge border (gamma) absorbs radiation, as an open space would. Things to try: the seed brush adds light (in phase with nothing — watch what it does to nearby solitons); s lower (≈0.1) makes solitons tighter and collisions more violent; g negative makes the medium defocusing (everything disperses); timestep up to ≈0.2 stays stable.",
